WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.140
Good morning, John

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:03.280
I kinda feel like that we've been really fancy on vlogbrothers lately

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:08.640
(We've) Got music video, big epic collab video, travel vlogs, tax plans, brazil video...

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.580
... you did a review of Monopoly

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:11.720
Let's simplify for a while

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.180
I'm just going to tell a story

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:15.000
A couple of years ago I was in the Bay area

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.500
I landed and I was going to a hotel that was a while away

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.400
so I got in a taxi and it took me to the hotel

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.500
The taxi driver was super nice

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.560
He asked me where I was from, we talked about Montana

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.500
He asked me why I was there, we talked about why i was there

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:28.420
... which is that I was doing a thing for the launch of Randell Munroe's book "What If"...

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:29.200
... which is a fantastic book

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:30.920
I even read to him a little bit from the book...

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:34.400
... because I just think "What If" is such a great thing to share with people.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.460
But, like a paragraph in, the taxi driver

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:37.400
.. he's like "Oh No!"

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.400
So then I'm also like "Oh what oh what happened?!"

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:43.100
... and he tells me that the cable that connects the meter to the transmission ...

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.100
has fallen out, meaning it hadn't recorded the first part of our journey

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.200
and if he plugged it in now then he'd only get paid for half.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.540
But he said he'd be fine because he does this trip a lot

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:51.440
He knows how much it costs

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:52.200
So he'll just charge me that

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.300
we get to the hotel and he tells me $120

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.800
And I say, "Okay"

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.200
And then I go to bed, and I get up in the morning

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.000
I do the event. It's great. And then..

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.000
I get another taxi, from that same hotel, to the airport

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.500
and its $65.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:06.000
And then I realized

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.200
Like it didn't even occur to me!

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:09.500
But yeah

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:11.200
And of course, immediately I'm like

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.100
... that guy was only nice to me, because he wanted to...

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.100
...know where I was from

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.100
And he trying to make it seem like

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:17.700
... there is no way this could be the kind of guy

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:18.700
... that would take advantage of me

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:19.600
He's so nice

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:20.600
I think about all the ways

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.300
in which I could have protected myself

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:23.840
from that, like so many..

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.380
common sense little things that I could have done

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.780
to prevent myself from getting taken advantage of in that way.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:33.600
Mostly just being ashamed that I was such like a country bumpkin rube.

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.280
That I was this guys mark, I was the patsy.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:40.240
And it made me feel like an idiot for trusting someone

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.240
and not being more skeptical.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:46.240
This one little example of the world like not always being totally on my side

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:49.120
And I start rethinking my entire, like

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.480
policy of being intentionally over-trusting.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:54.080
Which is a thing that actually think about.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.240
I want to be that.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:57.820
So this guy definitely stole like 50-60 bucks from me

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:00.880
but that, by far, was not the greatest, like,

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.260
negative impact that this experience had on me.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:06.180
Suddenly I'm thinking, like, how do I avoid getting taken advantage of again?

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:11.220
All the space in my brain that my new like, theft protection algorithms are going to take up

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:13.460
And just generally building a wall between me

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.620
and the world that's gonna decrease my enjoyment of the world.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:21.440
And this, from me, a guy who's pretty much got the whole world on his side

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.480
I'm a straight white dude with money

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:25.620
Just one little betrayal and I'm rethinking

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.120
the way I think about all other humans?

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:28.820
Come on!

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:31.980
Maybe every act of trust is a gamble that we choose to make.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.200
But I think the feeling of being taken advantage of

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:37.360
is way less pleasant than the feeling of having your

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:41.600
trust be justified and having the good, desired outcome happen.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.160
On the scale of violations of trust, of  course,

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.140
a taxi driver stealing your money and you not being smart enough to, like, realize it's happening

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:50.820
is reeaally far down the scale.

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:54.360
I don't want to say this was like a huge, terrible thing that happened to me.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.220
I'm just surprised the impact it had on me

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:59.580
and I feel like if I let that change the way I think about the world

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:04.260
that's not bad for the scammer, or the thief,

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:04.800
that's bad for me.

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:07.840
But if that guy is rationalizing his theft -

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.540
or if anybody is rationalizing them taking advantage of other people

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:16.240
by saying, like, "yeah that person can afford to have that stuff taken from them (whatever that is)."

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:17.900
Maybe, maybe that's the case

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:21.020
Maybe I can afford to have $60 stolen from me.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:26.080
But I don't think our society can afford to be constantly looking over its shoulder.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:27.740
We have to be able to trust each other

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:29.240
or society falls apart.

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:32.600
That to me is the true, untold, unrecognized cost

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:35.660
of being the kind of person who takes advantage of other people's trust.

00:03:35.660 --> 00:03:40.400
That thing, not the theft, is what ends up making the world a significantly worse place.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.980
And it makes me really angry.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:44.540
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:46.040
Hello, there, end screen.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:49.100
If you're interested in being a part of a street team for my upcoming tour

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:50.400
(dates in the description)

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.320
there's a link the the description to a form that you can fill out.

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:54.440
It would be great if you wanted to help us out.

